// Call by Reference: void f(int &r);`,options:["Call by Reference requires explicit dereferencing (*r) inside the function","Call by Address uses pointer syntax (&var and *ptr), while Call by Reference uses cleaner reference syntax","Call by Address creates a copy of the actual object","Call by Reference accepts NULL arguments"],correctAnswer:1,explanation:"Call by Address uses explicit pointers (`*`) and memory addresses (`&`), whereas Call by Reference provides cleaner alias syntax without needing explicit dereference operators."},{id:"u1s2q11",unitId:"unit1",setId:"u1s2",difficulty:"easy",question:"What is the output of the following code snippet?",code:`#include <iostream>
